it's great to welcome to the program  today democratic congressman jamie  raskin who represents maryland's 8th  congressional district was the lead  house manager in the second impeachment  trial of former president donald trump  congressman it's so great to have you on  i i'm delighted to be with you david  thank you for having me so okay to start  with what we're going to have starting  with in prime time on june 9th  uh these public hearings related to the  january 6 committee  and there are sort of two opposing  forces here there's one side yourself  included who have been saying what's  going to be revealed during those  hearings is going to be  mind-blowing that's my term not  necessarily a term that you have used  the other side is this feeling that  nothing's really going to happen  nobody's ultimately going to be held  accountable for what happened  what can you say to those in my audience  who are in that ladder group who are  concerned about lack of accountability  um well  this is all part of the process of  making  um  the events of january 6th visible  comprehensible to people and making them  the basis of real accountability now  some of that accountability is legal  accountability uh and that's really  within the realm of the department of  justice  which has brought more than 800  prosecutions in the most sweeping  investigation in the history of the doj  obviously we're all on the edge of our  seats to see if they're going to work  their way all the way up to the top or  whether uh trump as uh a kind of mob  boss is able to insulate himself with  several uh layers of buffer between him  and the criminal actions and the legal  consequences of those actions but i do  think that the investigation is  proceeding a pace and they're working  their way up the way that a lot of  organized crime investigations  work their way up uh the latter but then  there's there's public and political  accountability and that's really what  the january six select committee is all  about i mean under house resolution 503  our job is to report to congress and to  the american people about what happened  on january 6 and what were the causes  behind it and then to make  recommendations about how to fortify our  institutions going forward  do you expect some of your colleagues in  the house may have legal liability  well  um  you know  first of all that again that's not  really our role in other words we're  understanding we are turning and we're  turning over evidence of people who  are  uh  of congress because  they refuse to participate for example  um  dan scavino and peter navarro  you know they never  gave a single document to the committee  they never spent one minute testifying  they just blew us off  um  mark meadows was kind of one foot in and  one foot out doing the hokey pokey gave  us some stuff and then trump got mad  withdrew  um  you know steve bannon's blown us off  we've turned all of that information  over to the department of justice we've  voted told them in contempt and now it's  up to the executive branch and the doj  to enforce that  in terms of evidence of other crimes  we're  in the process of laying them out there  um  and you know in the john eastman  litigation when uh judge carter  was dealing with eastman's  claim that he was somehow covered by  attorney-client privilege  um  the judge asked the question well was  there an attorney client of privilege  was it waived and um  does it even apply if it existed because  of  the crime fraud exception and we've said  there was no attorney-client privilege  if there was it was waived and  you know  in any event  it doesn't apply because all of these  crimes were committed and we set them  forth and judge carter basically adopted  what we had to say so we've not been shy  about  setting forth statutory uh offenses um  but look  as much as we all would like to see  donald trump and  uh others in his uh  inner echelon  uh held legally accountable  far more important is whether or not we  can solidify our democratic institutions  and keep  uh the engines of democracy turning i  mean that's just so much more important  than you know how donald trump  spends the remaining years of his life  whether it's behind bars or somewhere  else i care a lot more about whether or  not we are going to be able to bequeath  the democracy a working functional  democracy to our children and our  grandchildren because the autocrats the  dictators the despots are on the march  all over the world and  from  moscow to mar-a-lago they're working to  undermine democratic process  and to drain  life from our institutions and we've got  to make sure we've got something to give  our kids yeah to that end i mean it  seems very clear that at least to some  degree some of the people who are trying  to avoid coming before your committee  are trying to run out the clock knowing  that if republicans take the house in  november and are sworn in in january  this committee is is essentially over is  your plan to be done  long before that in order to prevent an  interruption before you're done with  your work as a result of the election  [Music]  to have our hearings begin tonight uh  there will be six hearings uh at least  that take place in the month of june um  there will be um  perhaps another hearing or two in the  future to deliver the recommendations of  the committee and there will be a report  and we're hoping to have all of that  done  um early in the fall  uh you know it's not just our committee  that ends with this congress everything  ends with this congress because  house of representatives  uh unlike the senate  is a new  newly constituted body every two years  you know we come to the end of the  congress and then there's a new congress  that forms the senate because of the  overlapping nature of their terms is a  continuing body they only lose one-third  of their members  or one-third of their members are up for  re-election whereas our entire body's up  for re-election we adopt new rules at  the beginning of the house and so on so  it's not any kind of strategic political  calculus it's just that we've got to get  this done before uh this session of  congress ends okay when it comes to gun  safety there have been some of your  colleagues who have said something feels  different this time we've seen a couple  of republicans  including adam kinzinger and uh  congressman jacobs who have said i  actually have a new position when it  comes to either an assault weapons ban  or ar-15  there's something that that might be a  little bit different this time but we've  seen this cycle for a long time which is  there's a mass shooting everybody says  we must do something republicans come in  and say oh yeah sure but that can  include actually dealing with gun safety  and then there's no major shooting in  the news for a couple of weeks and  nothing happens and it's rinse repeat  for year after year after year is your  genuine opinion that this is going to be  that same cycle or that maybe something  will be different this time  well  you know my my colleagues senior to me  believe it is going to be the exact same  thing it's been before because the nra  exerts such a stranglehold over  the gop and  in the final analysis as they will  refuse to move uh on the other hand um  yeah i'm of the mind that uh  the republicans think that they are in  a place where they can actually  seize control of the house and the  senate and they can  win these elections um you know they've  obviously been uh vilifying and  demonizing joe biden and  uh yet they're starting to realize that  the public is overwhelmingly against  them on all the big issues i mean you  know they are the dog that caught the  car when it comes to destroying road  versus wade in the constitutional right  to privacy so that creates uh explosive  political conditions where the women of  america can turn all math against the  gop and what they've done to destroy  women's reproductive freedom um  similarly  uh the  the gun violence is so out of control  and so  dangerous and frightening to people  that there could they could experience a  similar  [Music]  earthquake with young people in america  who just say enough is enough and  the young people  who  really rocked american politics from  parkland florida  with the march for our lives  are  organizing another march  and people are saying that hundreds of  thousands or maybe even a million people  could come to washington um so i think  that the gop may have the sense  that uh their big chance to do what they  really care about most which is taking  power  could all be squandered  because of the recklessness of their  policies and so that would argue that  they might try to do something  so that they're not so clearly the party  that is tagged with all these massacres  one of my constituents wrote me the  other day with the idea that the  massacres  should be named after the republican  members of congress and the senators  whose districts and states  they represent when those are the  members who are doing nothing  to  get us a universal violent criminal  background check doing nothing to  close the  uh  you know the gun show loophole the  internet loophole the private seal  loophole doing nothing uh to take  weapons of war out of the hands of 18  year olds and  um  you know essentially reduce the  slaughter which puts america into a  class completely of its own i mean you  know we're suffering uh gun casualties  20 times 25 50 times higher um than  other  industrialized countries and it's all  because you know on that note um  many of my liberal friends who are for  all sorts of different gun safety  regulations which i know you support and  which i support  also are of the mindset that they don't  love the idea of only the right wingers  having the guns and they are  increasingly seeking firearms themselves  and obtaining them almost like a race to  the bottom of sorts if you will  do  do you sympathize with that fear does  that does that make sense to you to some  degree  well  i mean the supreme court of course in  heller versus district columbia said  that the core of the individual right  under the second amendment is the right  of self-defense in your home and um  people have that right that's deeply  anchored in american culture and law  and um  so  if there are people who  uh are fearful  for that reason and they they feel that  firearms will keep them safe uh they you  know they have a right to exercise their  second amendment rights um in that way  of course the liberals in the supreme  court had advanced the traditional view  which is that uh  the the second amendment is about the  right uh to possess guns in connection  with militia service in other words you  read the prefatory clause or  well-regulated militia being necessary  of the survival of the free state  um comma in conjunction with the  operative clause the right of the people  to keep and bear arms shall not be in  french in any of it the conservative  majority won on that but even justice  scalia uh was emphatic  that  reasonable gun regulation is totally  within the contemplation of the second  amendment and you don't have to sell  assault weapons to  uh  anybody and you don't have to allow kids  to get weapons and you don't have to  allow people to take weapons into public  places um you know i i hope we have not  arrived at civil war style conditions  um but i you know i think it's perfectly  healthy for people across the political  spectrum to exercise their second  amendment rights but it's also perfectly  healthy for us to understand what second  second amendment rights really are right  and what the authority of government is  to guarantee public safety because  that's essential to the social contract  and the whole reason we get into a  social contract if you read locke and  hobbes and rousseau is that we  surrender the rights of violent  self-help  in exchange for  security in a civil society uh but the  republicans who refuse to do nothing  essentially you know give us the worst  of both worlds they compel kids to go to  school under the truancy laws under the  mandatory attendance laws but then they  leave them as sitting ducks because they  won't  do anything to  keep them safe from mass slaughter with  assault weapons  congressman last thing i want to ask you  about and i know our time is is not  unlimited i'm not an advocate of a  primary challenge to joe biden i think  if joe biden decides to run for  re-election that he should be the  democratic nominee from a strategic  point of view that being said when  people say to me  david is there anyone you think would be  interesting to see as the democratic  nominee to be totally honest i think of  you i think of john ossoff i think john  ossoff would be very interesting i know  that it would be  a crazy thing to ask you if it's  something you think about thinking about  you would never answer it it would be  inappropriate it would be a crazy thing  to ask but my more specific question is  one of the things i do think is i think  that  unfortunately because of anti-semitism  on both the right and some on the left  i think jewish presidential candidates  would still struggle because of  anti-semitism what's your opinion about  that issue in america right now  i hadn't really thought that through  david uh well let me see um and i i  wonder if you're asking me this because  i i happen to be in new hampshire  campaigning for my friends uh chris  pappas and annie kuster and for senator  maggie hassan uh but um  you know  look  i think it's really important for the  young people of america to understand  and the kids of america to understand  despite what they might see on the  internet and despite what they might  hear from some people in public office  we are not a racist country we are not  an anti-semitic country we are not a  sexist misogynistic  country we are not an immigrant fashion  country and  um i believe uh so much in you know what  tom payne saw when he got to america i  mean he said this country  could become an asylum for humanity not  an insane asylum mind you but it could  become a place of refuge for people  fleeing from every form of political  religious economic repression and  discrimination on earth  and i think despite what we've lived  with through donald trump and the proud  boys and the oath keepers and the  militia groups and the three percenters  and despite all of the divisiveness and  the polarization i still believe we are  the world's greatest multi-racial  multi-religious multi-ethnic  constitutional democracy and the sky's  the limit for what we can make happen  for our people once we fight off all of  these authoritarian racist attacks  on our country so  um  you know there there would be a lot of  reasons that i wouldn't run for  president but being jewish would not be  one of them  congressman jamie raskin i really  appreciate your time and we're going to  be watching uh next week as the hearings  start very closely thank you so much  thanks so much for having me david

